2026-10-31 14:58 Design pass done (ocp-choices-worker, read-only, nothing changed, no tests run; base19). Full doc: scratchpad w-choices306/design.md.
Decision: no validate-first gate on the read side. name, control_planes and workers refuse in validate()'s words, as kubernetes/gpu/ssh/system/robocop/switches already do since k331/k340/k355: die "...\n", not croak. Every reader of those three treats each entry as a mapping, so refusing the whole list on one bad entry refuses exactly what is read. The deeper shapes (an entry's host/public_ip/name/provider; a pool's nodes/host) come from one public OCP::Config::spec_errors(@paths). It returns validate()'s lines per key path (name, control_planes, control_planes[I|*], control_planes[I].provider/host/public_ip/..., workers, workers[I|*], workers[I].name/provider/nodes/host/..., optionally gpu for k333). Unknown keys, required lines and bounds stay validate()-only (k347/k355). validate() calls the same private producers at today's spots, so its order is kept; a property test pins spec_errors as an ordered sub-list of validate(). Status reads each spec section in an eval: the line goes to STDERR once and the report goes on (the _amd_gpu_default_allocation precedent); OCP::Drift turns a refusal into a kind=>error entry. Destroy keeps its own selection (k323/k335/k345); only the line text moves to Config.
Plan: 1 Config spec_errors with no behaviour change (choices); 2a Destroy (destroy), 2b Machines (secrets), 2c node add gpu (apply, optional); 3 one batch: accessors refuse (choices) + Status/Version (status) + Drift (state) + node ls/ssh/kubeconfig (ocp-worker; no lane names them) + Init (apply) + provider add (test only). Step 3 replaces t/230's ssh-pool count claim (k339).
k361: land first, narrowly. worker_ocpnodes reads nodes: as a count only when it is a whole number within a bound, else as one machine, as control_planes() does. Not 'every caller gates'.
Also found (for the dispatcher):
F1 validate() has no upper bound on a pool's nodes: count. nodes: 1000000000000 on a hetzner or local pool passes validate() and OOMs ocp apply in worker_ocpnodes. k361 assumes a bound that does not exist; the number is a maintainer decision.
F2 ocp node ls --json OOMs on P1-P4 (_pools_of -> worker_ocpnodes; its eval does not stop that). k361 does not list it, and status does not call worker_ocpnodes.
F3 the refusing accessors croak, so OCP::run_cli labels them 'internal error' where nothing gates first (e.g. ocp update with gpu: false, Update.pm:125->303; by reading).
F4 network/registry/cilium/ssl_config return raw values; network: foo dies inside Perl in node add, deploy-robocop and update.
F5 ocp provider add with name: [a] writes clusterName ARRAY(0x...) into the OCPNodeProvider, so destroy misses robocop's servers (k98 class).
F6 destroy's fallback (Destroy.pm:253) and Machines (:157) read public_ip without a shape check; steps 2a/2b close it.
Card stays in-progress for the implementation, after k353/k367/k361/k374 land.

